<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Franz Ferdinand · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg-lorraine">House of Habsburg-Lorraine</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Archduke Franz Ferdinand was the nephew of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> and heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne from 1896. He favoured army reform and greater rights for the empire's Slav peoples, and his morganatic marriage to Sophie Chotek strained relations at court. His assassination with his wife in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> on 28 June 1914 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavrilo-princip">Gavrilo Princip</a> set off the crisis that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria.jpg" alt="Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ferdinand Schmutzer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinand_Schmutzer_-_Franz_Ferdinand_von_%C3%96sterreich-Este,_um_1914_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Franz Ferdinand was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> on 18 December 1863, the eldest son of Archduke Karl Ludwig, the younger brother of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a>, and of his second wife, Princess Maria Annunziata of Bourbon-Two Sicilies. In 1875, when he was eleven, his cousin Francis V, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, died and named him his heir on condition that he add the name Este to his own. The inheritance made him one of the wealthiest men in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><p>In 1889 Crown Prince Rudolf committed suicide at Mayerling, which left Karl Ludwig first in line to the throne. When Karl Ludwig died of typhoid fever in 1896, Franz Ferdinand became heir presumptive. Between 1892 and 1893 he travelled round the world, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. He was an excessive trophy hunter, even by the standards of European nobility; his diaries record 272,511 game kills, and about 100,000 trophies were displayed at his Bohemian castle at Konopiště.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Like most males in the ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> line, he entered the army young, and was promoted rapidly: lieutenant at fourteen, captain at twenty-two, colonel at twenty-seven and major general at thirty-one. He never received formal staff training but briefly led the largely Hungarian 9th Hussar Regiment. In 1898 he was commissioned to inquire into all aspects of the military services, and in 1913 he was appointed inspector general of the Austro-Hungarian Armed Forces. He received the rank of admiral in 1902. His relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> were tense. His courtship of Sophie Chotek, a lady-in-waiting, caused conflict in the imperial household, and their morganatic marriage in 1900 was allowed only after he renounced his descendants' rights to the throne.</p><p>Historians disagree on how to characterise his politics: some attribute generally liberal views on the empire's nationalities, others stress his dynastic centralism and Catholic conservatism. He advocated greater autonomy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and the south Slavic peoples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, who had been left out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a>, but felt antipathy towards the Hungarians, whose nationalism he saw as a threat to the dynasty. He urged caution towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and clashed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-conrad-von-hotzendorf">Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf</a>, the Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>, warning that harsh treatment of Serbia would bring conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and ruin both empires.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 28 June 1914 Franz Ferdinand and his wife were assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavrilo-princip">Gavrilo Princip</a>, aged nineteen, a member of Young Bosnia. The killing led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-crisis">July Crisis</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>'s declaration of war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Four weeks after his death the allies of Austria-Hungary and the allies of Serbia declared war on each other, which began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. His assassination is regarded as the most immediate cause of the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The assassination of the heir to the throne, <b>Archduke Franz Ferdinand</b>, in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914 by the Bosnian Serb nationalist Gavrilo Princip triggered the July Crisis that produced the First World War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 28 June 1914 Archduke <b>Franz Ferdinand</b> was assassinated in Sarajevo, an act behind which stood the secret Serbian officers' organisation Black Hand, which aimed to break the South Slav provinces away from Austria-Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>One month after the assassination of Archduke <b>Franz Ferdinand</b>, Austria-Hungary declared war on Serbia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-28">28 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On 28 June 1914 Gavrilo Princip assassinated Archduke <b>Franz Ferdinand</b> in Sarajevo.</blockquote><a href="/state/bosnia-and-herzegovina-austro-hungarian">Bosnia and Herzegovina (Austro-Hungarian)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.07,15.44&amp;year=1896" aria-label="Graz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4912 511 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5362 781h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5362 781h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.07,15.44&amp;year=1896">What was here in 1896? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
